arbtt, the Automatic Rule-Based Time Tracker

(c) 2009-2026 Joachim Breitner and contributors

The Automatic Rule-Based Time Tracker is a desktop daemon that runs in the background and, every minute, records what windows are open on your desktop, what their titles are, which one is active. The accompanied statistics program lets you derive information from this log file, i.e.  what how much of your time have you been spending with e-Mail, or what projects are your largest time wasters. The mapping from the raw window titles to sensible "tags" is done by a configuration file with an powerful syntax.

At this point, arbtt does not have a graphical user interface. You will see no indication that arbtt-capture is running besides its entry in the task manager.  The statistics can be accessed using the arbtt-stats program from the command line.

The programs use UTF-8 for text output. Windows Terminal and recent PowerShell
versions display it correctly by default. In legacy cmd.exe, run `chcp 65001`
before using the reporting tools if non-ASCII text is displayed incorrectly.

Installation
============

This installation puts the programs and an example configuration in your user
profile. By default it starts arbtt-capture after installation and whenever you
sign in. It can also add the arbtt program directory to your user PATH, so that
you can run arbtt-stats from Windows Terminal, PowerShell, or cmd.exe. These
options can be changed on the installer's task page.

The online documentation and the file categorize.cfg can be opened from the
Start menu.

You can uninstall arbtt from Windows Settings or its Start menu shortcut. The
installer asks Windows to close arbtt-capture when upgrading or uninstalling.

Third-party component notice
